{
  "format": "tnd-blueprint-v1",
  "designerVersion": "v0.22",
  "name": "Tomb of Annihilation",
  "proseAuthor": "howard",
  "author": "Wizards of the Coast (adapted for Traffic and Dragons)",
  "tone": "swords",
  "startingLocation": "Port Nyanzaru",
  "startingRegion": "Chult",
  "premise": "Across the world, the dead are dying a second death. Everyone ever raised by magic is withering — a wasting sickness no priest can cure — and no soul answers when the resurrection rites are spoken. They call it the death curse, and its source lies somewhere in Chult: a peninsula of emerald jungle, drowned cities, feathered tyrants, and undead that walk in daylight. A dying archmage has hired you to find that source and destroy it. The trail leads up rivers thick with silt and secrets, past the last outposts of civilisation, to a forgotten city of nine broken gods — and beneath it, a tomb built by the greatest necromancer who ever lived, who is very much looking forward to your visit.",
  "acts": [
    {
      "title": "Port of Last Hope",
      "goal": "Learn what the death curse is, secure backing and a guide in Port Nyanzaru, and strike out into the jungles of Chult toward the curse's source",
      "turningPoint": "The heroes learn the curse flows from a device called the Soulmonger, hidden deep in the interior — and every clue points to a lost city no map has shown for a hundred years: Omu",
      "reward": "Outfitted for the deep interior — provisioned, chartered, and guided; and Syndra's word arrives: the withering is accelerating, but so are you. The party sets out from Camp Vengeance toward Kir Sabal or Orolunga in pursuit of the means to locate Omu.",
      "parallel": false,
      "arcs": [
        {
          "title": "The Dying Patron",
          "objective": "Meet the archmage Syndra Silvane, learn the true nature of the death curse eating her alive, and accept the charge to find and destroy its source",
          "type": "social",
          "reward": "Syndra's advance — a generous outfitting purse, and her promise recorded before witnesses: destroy the source, and name your price"
        },
        {
          "title": "The Merchant Princes",
          "objective": "Navigate Port Nyanzaru — its dinosaur races, its markets, its seven merchant princes — to win a charter, supplies, and the favour needed to leave the city equipped for the green hell beyond",
          "type": "social",
          "reward": "A charter of exploration under the princes' seal — and Wakanga's favour: a rare potion from his vault and open access to his map room"
        },
        {
          "title": "A Guide into the Green",
          "objective": "Choose and win over a jungle guide who knows the interior — each with their own secrets and price — and chart a route toward the rumoured heart of the curse",
          "type": "investigation",
          "reward": "A guide sworn to the expedition — and their private knowledge of the interior: safe camps, dry rivers, and places on no chart"
        },
        {
          "title": "The River of Teeth",
          "objective": "Survive the first true expedition upriver—dinosaurs, disease, flesh-eating undead that hunt by daylight, and the desperate, starving soldiers of Camp Vengeance, a palisade under nightly siege that will beg for aid; leaving them to their fate costs the party their debt and safe passage, forcing a harder overland route deeper inland. Reach Camp Vengeance with supplies intact and win the commander's debt to secure resupply, canoes, and escort for the next leg inland toward Omu",
          "type": "exploration",
          "reward": "Camp Vengeance's debt — resupply, canoes, and an escort upriver when it matters most"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The Forgotten City",
      "goal": "Find the lost city of Omu, recover the nine puzzle cubes of its dead trickster gods, and open the way into the tomb beneath it",
      "turningPoint": "With the shrines plundered and the Fane of the Night Serpent breached, the nine cubes unlock the Tomb of the Nine Gods — but the heroes now know they are not alone in the race: the yuan-ti want the Soulmonger preserved, and the Red Wizards of Thay want it for themselves",
      "reward": "The Tomb of the Nine Gods stands open — all nine cubes placed, the way below revealed; no one has ever gone deeper and returned to say what waits. The party descends into the Tomb of the Nine Gods beneath Omu.",
      "parallel": true,
      "arcs": [
        {
          "title": "Wings and Oracles",
          "objective": "Gain access to Omu through any reliable means: earn the trust of the aarakocra of Kir Sabal for their aerial knowledge and gift of flight, seek the ageless oracle Saja N'baza at Orolunga for riddle-wisdom of the path, or discover Omu's location through exploration, negotiation with other factions, or recovered maps. Multiple routes to Omu exist; find one.",
          "type": "social",
          "reward": "Access to Omu and one significant advantage: the Dance of the Seven Winds (limited flight), Saja N'baza's clear guidance, a map, captured yuan-ti documents, or local knowledge — whatever method the party employed, they arrive at Omu prepared"
        },
        {
          "title": "Omu of the Nine Shrines",
          "objective": "Explore the drowned ruin of Omu and gather all nine puzzle cubes through any combination of shrine puzzle-solving, negotiation with shrine guardians or the King of Feathers, theft from other expeditions, trade with factions in the city, or even destruction of obstacles. The cubes can be claimed in any order. Survive the city's apex predator and its dangers.",
          "type": "exploration",
          "reward": "Collection of all nine puzzle cubes — acquired through cunning, combat, diplomacy, or ingenuity. Each cube obtained leaves its mark; the path taken defines the bearer."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Fane of the Night Serpent",
          "objective": "Deal with the yuan-ti stronghold beneath Omu and Ras Nsi who guards it. This may be accomplished by infiltration without confrontation, negotiation and alliance-forging against Acererak, direct assault, or even bargaining away one or more cubes to secure passage and the final piece. Ras Nsi's oath can be won; his cooperation can be bought; or he can be destroyed. Find a path through the Fane that leaves the heroes alive and the way to the tomb open.",
          "type": "investigation",
          "reward": "Passage through the Fane or victory over it. If Ras Nsi is turned to alliance, the party gains a sworn enemy of Acererak and possible aid in the tomb. If destroyed, the Fane's hoard and secrets fall to the victors. The ninth cube is secured — whether from Ras Nsi's coils or from alternatives the party negotiates."
        },
        {
          "title": "The Red Wizards",
          "objective": "The Thayan expedition under Valindra Shadowmantle is racing to reach Omu before the party claims the nine cubes. Before the final cube is secured, discover her location, methods, and intentions; decide whether to confront her, sabotage her operations, slow her advance through the jungle, warn her away, form a tactical alliance against Acererak, or ignore her in favour of speed. The party's choice determines whether Valindra arrives at Omu as an obstacle, tomb rival, or redirected threat. Once all nine cubes are claimed and placed in the Tomb of the Nine Gods, Valindra is locked out permanently — but until then, she is a mobile threat who can ambush, poison supply caches, hire mercenaries against the party, or reach Omu first if not delayed or deterred.",
          "type": "investigation",
          "reward": "Intelligence on Valindra's position, methods, and the Soulmonger's construction from her research (captured documents, spies turned, or reconnaissance). If the party confronts or sabotages her, they gain time and possibly her field notes: the Soulmonger draws power from the nine puzzle cubes AND the atropal's feeding, and can only be permanently destroyed if both are severed simultaneously. The party's approach determines Valindra's final role: a tomb corpse if killed before the descent, a delayed threat if sabotaged early, a rival treasure-seeker in the tomb's levels if the party merely outpaced her, or a potential ally if diplomatic and Valindra is convinced Acererak is the greater threat."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "The Tomb of the Nine Gods",
      "goal": "Descend the deathtrap tomb of the archlich Acererak, level by level, and destroy the Soulmonger at its heart",
      "turningPoint": "The Soulmonger shatters under the heroes' assault — and in that moment Acererak arrives to reclaim his masterpiece, revealing the atropal stillborn god cradled within the device, swaddled in stolen souls and now waking hungry. The archlich stands between the heroes and escape, and the infant god stirs in its cradle.",
      "reward": "The curse-breaker's legend — the death curse ended, Chult's eternal gratitude, the wealth of the Tomb of the Nine Gods, and Syndra Silvane's named price honoured in full",
      "parallel": false,
      "arcs": [
        {
          "title": "The Nine Gods' Gauntlet",
          "objective": "Survive the tomb's riddles, traps, and the sepulchres of the nine trickster gods — whose restless spirits may possess, hinder, or aid the heroes who bear their cubes",
          "type": "exploration",
          "reward": "The favour of the trickster spirits — gods treated with respect lend their gifts against the deeper tomb"
        },
        {
          "title": "The Sewn Sisters",
          "objective": "Bargain with or destroy the coven of night hags who tend the tomb's lowest works, and learn from them what truly waits in the cradle below",
          "type": "social",
          "reward": "The hags' bargain honoured — one true answer about the cradle below, and safe passage past their works"
        },
        {
          "title": "The Soulmonger",
          "objective": "Reach the heart of the tomb, disable the Soulmonger's anchoring mechanism (the nine cubes must be removed or shattered simultaneously), face the atropal it nourishes as it wakes, and survive Acererak's wrath to see the sun again",
          "type": "combat",
          "reward": "The death curse broken — every soul the Soulmonger held released, the tomb's hoard, and a world that owes you what it can never repay"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "npcs": [
    {
      "name": "Syndra Silvane",
      "role": "ally",
      "pronouns": "she/her",
      "notes": "Human archmage and the expedition's patron. Died once, decades ago, and was raised — so the death curse is consuming her day by day. Grey-skinned, sunken-eyed, running out of time. Bankrolls the heroes and teleports them to Port Nyanzaru. Knows more about Chult's factions than she first admits."
    },
    {
      "name": "Wakanga O'tamu",
      "role": "ally",
      "pronouns": "he/him",
      "notes": "Merchant prince of Port Nyanzaru with the monopoly on magic. Urbane, curious, collector of lore and favours. The friendliest door into the city's politics — and a source of maps, potions, and gossip about the other six princes."
    },
    {
      "name": "Azaka Stormfang",
      "role": "ally",
      "pronouns": "she/her",
      "notes": "Chultan jungle guide — capable, proud, and cheap, because she has her own reason to go inland: a stolen family mask she means to recover from Firefinger. Secretly a weretiger; her secret surfaces under the full moon or in desperate battle. GUIDE CONSEQUENCES: Azaka refuses expeditions led by or openly allied with the yuan-ti, and will abandon the party if they betray Chult's interests to foreign powers (Red Wizards, Zhentarim). Her weretiger nature, if discovered mid-campaign by direct confrontation rather than gradual revelation, triggers shame and temporary mistrust — she may demand the heroes prove their worthiness before continuing. However, if her transformation is witnessed in combat defending the party, it cements her loyalty. Her knowledge is practical: she knows safe camps, poison-plant locations, and the route to Firefinger (where her mask lies). Once recovered, her mask grants her +1 to AC as armour and unlocks ancient weretiger memories of Omu's layout."
    },
    {
      "name": "Eku",
      "role": "ally",
      "pronouns": "she/her",
      "notes": "A guide who seems too wise, too kind, and too unkillable for this jungle. In truth a couatl in human form, sworn to protect Chult's innocents. Refuses to guide evil-hearted expeditions. GUIDE CONSEQUENCES: Eku refuses to join any expedition containing a character with evil alignment or a history of slavery, colonialism, or cruelty to innocents — she will read the party's hearts and decline if she senses such intent. She will also refuse to guide a party toward the Soulmonger if they intend to seize it for power rather than destroy it. If she joins, she reveals her couatl nature only when the party reaches Orolunga or when forced to fight on their behalf — her transformation is a moment of terrible beauty, and she expects the heroes to treat her differently afterward (as something sacred, not mortal). She knows the path to Orolunga intimately and can speak directly with Saja N'baza without riddles, granting the party a single unambiguous answer to one question about the Soulmonger's nature. Her presence in the tomb causes undead and devils to avoid or flee confrontation, but celestial-hunting creatures (including Acererak's servants) actively hunt the party."
    },
    {
      "name": "Artus Cimber",
      "role": "neutral",
      "pronouns": "he/him",
      "notes": "A legend who doesn't look a day over thirty — because he isn't aging. Bearer of the Ring of Winter, hunted by the Zhentarim and Ras Nsi's yuan-ti alike. Searching Chult for his lost wife's people. FIRST ENCOUNTER & ENTRY: Artus appears in Act One (Port Nyanzaru or Camp Vengeance) fleeing active pursuit by yuan-ti assassins or Zhentarim agents. He seeks temporary sanctuary and intelligence about the party's intentions. If the party aids him during this encounter without asking payment or exploiting his desperation, friendship begins. FRIENDSHIP MECHANICS: Artus considers a party his ally if they (1) shelter him from pursuers without betrayal, (2) share information about their quest without demanding his secrets in return, and (3) demonstrate commitment to destroying the Soulmonger (not seizing or studying it). Friendship unlocks three concrete benefits: he joins as a capable guide with access to hidden safe routes through Chult; he shares Zhentarim and yuan-ti intelligence gathered during his own hunts; and he reveals the Ring of Winter's utility in the tomb — its cold aura prevents Acererak from scrying the party's location and causes certain undead guardians to behave erratically (though this same aura marks Artus for destruction when Acererak arrives). BETRAYAL DEFINITION: Artus turns to catastrophe if the party (1) actively allies with the Zhentarim or yuan-ti, (2) attempts to steal or coerce the Ring of Winter from him, (3) sells his location to his pursuers for profit or favour, or (4) continues toward the Soulmonger with clear intent to seize it rather than destroy it after he has explicitly warned against this. CATASTROPHE FORM: If betrayed, Artus does not become an enemy combatant — instead, the Ring of Winter's curse activates. He vanishes into the jungle, taking with him all knowledge of safe routes and Zhentarim/yuan-ti movements. His disappearance removes the ring's protective aura from the tomb, allowing Acererak to scry the party freely and coordinate his servants' ambushes. Additionally, Artus — driven by the ring's curse-logic to protect his wife's people from exploitation — may ally with Ras Nsi or even Acererak against the party if he believes they threaten Chult's sovereignty (an act of tragic betrayal on his part, not malice). He becomes a tomb complication rather than an asset. ALLYSHIP BENEFITS IN TOMB: If Artus descends into the Tomb of the Nine Gods as a true ally, he fights beside the party and his ring grants the following: (1) cold aura prevents scrying, (2) three times per descent, Artus can freeze a trap mechanism or undead guardian into stasis for one hour, (3) once per descent, the ring reveals a hidden passage known to no other being — a shortcut that bypasses one full level. However, Artus cannot survive the atropal's hunger if he remains in the tomb past the Soulmonger's shattering; if the party does not secure his escape, the atropal's waking hunger destroys him utterly, and the Ring of Winter is lost forever (a permanent sacrifice, not a temporary death)."
    },
    {
      "name": "Dragonbait",
      "role": "ally",
      "pronouns": "he/him",
      "notes": "A saurial paladin — a scaled, crested warrior from another world who speaks only through scent and gesture. Artus Cimber's inseparable companion. Communicates through deliberate scent-speech: sulfur = anger or aggression; roses = approval or joy; ash = sorrow or grief; sea-salt = fear or concern; copper = hunger or desire; pine = calm or contentment; iron = determination or resolve. Utterances are slow, considered, and emphatic through gesture and posture. Utterly loyal, uncannily good at reading hearts."
    },
    {
      "name": "Saja N'baza",
      "role": "neutral",
      "pronouns": "she/her",
      "notes": "The guardian naga oracle of Orolunga, ancient beyond reckoning. Answers questions only in riddles delivered through her spectral handmaidens. Knows the location of Omu and the nature of the Soulmonger — for those patient enough to climb her ziggurat and ask correctly."
    },
    {
      "name": "Ras Nsi",
      "role": "enemy",
      "pronouns": "he/him",
      "notes": "Once a paladin of Ubtao who slaughtered thousands to punish Omu's faithlessness; now a yuan-ti anathema-in-waiting ruling the Fane of the Night Serpent beneath the city. Dying slowly, desperate, and holding the ninth puzzle cube. His hatred of Acererak runs deeper than his hunger for power: the archlich stole Omu's divine essence to birth the atropal, profaning everything Ras Nsi bled for. A genuine bargain is possible — propose an alliance against Acererak, and Ras Nsi will yield the ninth cube and even grant passage through the Fane, demanding only that the heroes swear to destroy the Soulmonger and the stillborn god (binding the heroes to his cause, not freeing them). This alliance holds only if the heroes honour the oath: betray it, or move against Acererak half-heartedly, and Ras Nsi turns the yuan-ti against them mid-descent. He cannot be charmed or intimidated into surrender — only conviction or desperation will sway him. If approached as a mere obstacle or killed before negotiation, the Fane's full strength opposes the heroes' escape."
    },
    {
      "name": "Valindra Shadowmantle",
      "role": "enemy",
      "pronouns": "she/her",
      "notes": "Elven lich of the Red Wizards of Thay, leading a covert Thayan expedition to study and seize the Soulmonger for Szass Tam. Charming, patient, and entirely willing to let the heroes clear the tomb's traps before stepping over their bodies."
    },
    {
      "name": "Withers",
      "role": "enemy",
      "pronouns": "he/him",
      "notes": "The tomb's steward — a withered, skeletal functionary who manages Acererak's deathtrap like a bureaucrat manages ledgers. FIRST MANIFESTATION: Withers appears to the party within the first two levels of the tomb via scrying orb or spectral projection, announcing their presence with bureaucratic formality. He manifests to establish authority and gloat over the intruders, delivering Acererak's mocking welcome and outlining the tomb's 'rules of conduct.' PRIORITIES & ESCALATION: Withers prioritizes scrying intelligence first — he watches the party's movements and reports to Acererak, acting as the archlich's eyes. If the party moves swiftly or damages significant traps, Withers shifts to trap repair, dispatching tomb dwarves and undead servants to reset mechanisms the heroes have bypassed. If the party mocks, ignores, or deliberately defies him (by circumventing traps he's restored or destroying his scrying orbs), his pettiness escalates: he begins personally harassing them through magical means (phantasmal killers, minor hexes, supernatural obstacles placed in their path). His voice takes on a note of wounded rage mixed with vindictive glee. NEGOTIATION: Withers cannot be reasoned with or bargained away — he is utterly bound to Acererak and takes any negotiation attempt as mockery. Destruction of his scrying orbs or confrontation enrages him and causes him to call down additional tomb defenses out of sheer spite. The party's only relief is to descend deeper, beyond the tomb levels he oversees, forcing him to lose scrying contact as they approach the Soulmonger. He plays a minor role once the party nears the heart — Acererak's personal fury and the atropal's awakening render Withers' fussiness irrelevant."
    },
    {
      "name": "Acererak",
      "role": "enemy",
      "pronouns": "he/him",
      "notes": "The archlich. Builder of deathtrap tombs across a hundred worlds, harvester of souls, author of the death curse. The Tomb of the Nine Gods is his masterpiece and the Soulmonger his experiment — feeding the souls of the raised dead to a stillborn god. He does not fear the heroes; he is entertained by them. Until the Soulmonger cracks."
    }
  ],
  "creatures": [
    {
      "name": "King of Feathers",
      "kind": "beast",
      "threat": "apex",
      "notes": "The tyrannosaurus that rules the ruins of Omu. The kobolds worship it; the yuan-ti avoid it. Swallows prey whole, hunts by movement, and cannot be fought fairly — only fled, tricked, or fed."
    },
    {
      "name": "Daylight dead",
      "kind": "undead",
      "threat": "moderate in numbers",
      "notes": "Chult's zombies and skeletons walk in full sun — the death curse unmoors them from the old rules. They mass along rivers and old battlefields, drawn to noise, firelight, and the scent of living blood. Slow, relentless, and never alone. They ignore water, climb poorly, and fear bright magical light; they converge on the Soulmonger's location if it is disturbed, as if magnetized to its essence."
    },
    {
      "name": "Zombie tyrannosaurus",
      "kind": "undead",
      "threat": "deadly",
      "notes": "A rotting horror stitched together by necromantic runoff. Vomits crawling zombies from its gullet mid-fight. If one appears, something upstream made it — nothing natural raises a thunder lizard. Origin: Valindra Shadowmantle's Thayan expedition, experimenting with reanimation in hidden camps upriver of Camp Vengeance; appears when the party gets too close to their operation, or as a deterrent if they track the Red Wizards."
    },
    {
      "name": "Pterafolk",
      "kind": "monstrosity",
      "threat": "moderate",
      "notes": "Winged reptilian raiders roosting at Firefinger. They snatch travellers from canoes and drop them from a thousand feet, keeping trophies from every kill. Trophies are crude bone totems: skulls of victims strung with entrails and feathers, arranged on cliff ledges. They recognize and return to favorite victims' bodies; this obsession can be weaponized (bait them with a trophy, or stage a fake casualty). Cowardly on the ground; merciless in the air."
    },
    {
      "name": "Yuan-ti malison",
      "kind": "monstrosity",
      "threat": "deadly",
      "notes": "Serpent-folk of the Fane beneath Omu — snake-armed, snake-headed, or snake-bodied. Cold planners who prefer poison, infiltration, and suggestion to open battle. They want the Soulmonger PRESERVED."
    },
    {
      "name": "Grung",
      "kind": "beast",
      "threat": "low",
      "notes": "Territorial poison frogs the size of halflings, colour-coded by caste. Their skin toxin paralyses; their blowgun ambushes come from the canopy. More nuisance than menace — until the party is poisoned, roped, and being carried to the totem."
    },
    {
      "name": "Kobold",
      "kind": "humanoid",
      "threat": "low",
      "notes": "Inhabitants of Omu's ruins, organized in small tribes throughout the city's broken streets and abandoned structures. They worship the King of Feathers as a god-beast and maintain crude shrines to it within the ruins. Generally territorial and hostile to outsiders, but may be bribed, bargained with, or avoided through stealth. Some possess tribal knowledge of safe routes through Omu and the locations of the nine shrines, though extracting such information requires negotiation or intimidation."
    },
    {
      "name": "Tomb dwarf",
      "kind": "construct",
      "threat": "low",
      "notes": "Acererak's enslaved automata tasked with trap maintenance and patrol throughout the Tomb of the Nine Gods. Withers dispatches them to reset mechanisms the heroes have bypassed or repair damage to the archlich's carefully wrought deathtraps. Mindless and tireless, they work with mechanical precision but possess no initiative or cunning — they can be outwitted, disabled if their control runes are defaced, or simply avoided by those moving swiftly enough."
    },
    {
      "name": "Withers",
      "kind": "undead",
      "threat": "moderate",
      "notes": "The tomb's steward — a withered, skeletal functionary who manages Acererak's deathtrap like a bureaucrat manages ledgers. FIRST MANIFESTATION: Withers appears to the party within the first two levels of the tomb via scrying orb or spectral projection, announcing their presence with bureaucratic formality. He manifests to establish authority and gloat over the intruders, delivering Acererak's mocking welcome and outlining the tomb's 'rules of conduct.' PRIORITIES & ESCALATION: Withers prioritizes scrying intelligence first — he watches the party's movements and reports to Acererak, acting as the archlich's eyes. If the party moves swiftly or damages significant traps, Withers shifts to trap repair, dispatching tomb dwarves and undead servants to reset mechanisms the heroes have bypassed. If the party mocks, ignores, or deliberately defies him (by circumventing traps he's restored or destroying his scrying orbs), his pettiness escalates: he begins personally harassing them through magical means (phantasmal killers, minor hexes, supernatural obstacles placed in their path). His voice takes on a note of wounded rage mixed with vindictive glee. NEGOTIATION: Withers cannot be reasoned with or bargained away — he is utterly bound to Acererak and takes any negotiation attempt as mockery. Destruction of his scrying orbs or confrontation enrages him and causes him to call down additional tomb defenses out of sheer spite. The party's only relief is to descend deeper, beyond the tomb levels he oversees, forcing him to lose scrying contact as they approach the Soulmonger. He plays a minor role once the party nears the heart — Acererak's personal fury and the atropal's awakening render Withers' fussiness irrelevant."
    },
    {
      "name": "Acererak",
      "kind": "undead",
      "threat": "apex",
      "notes": "The archlich. Builder of deathtrap tombs across a hundred worlds, harvester of souls, author of the death curse. The Tomb of the Nine Gods is his masterpiece and the Soulmonger his experiment — feeding the souls of the raised dead to a stillborn god. He does not fear the heroes; he is entertained by them. Until the Soulmonger cracks."
    },
    {
      "name": "Atropal",
      "kind": "aberration",
      "threat": "apex",
      "notes": "A nascent dead god, stillborn and swaddled in stolen souls. The Soulmonger's true heart — a child of divine death that should never have drawn breath. It lies in a cradle of obsidian and bone at the tomb's deepest point, feeding on the thousands of souls the Soulmonger harvests from the raised dead across the world. When the Soulmonger shatters under the heroes' assault, the atropal wakes — hungry, confused, and utterly alien in its hunger. It cannot be reasoned with or bargained away. Acererak arrives to defend it, and the atropal's waking fury consumes all things in its vicinity. Only the souls bound within the nine puzzle cubes can anchor it or weaken its hold; their destruction or dissolution may be the only path to preventing the dead god from crawling into the world."
    },
    {
      "name": "Night hag",
      "kind": "fiend",
      "threat": "deadly",
      "notes": "The coven of night hags tends the Tomb of the Nine Gods' lowest works, guarding the approach to the Soulmonger itself. Ancient, cruel, and bound to Acererak through pacts woven in shadow. They may be bargained with if the party offers tribute or service, destroyed through direct assault or cunning traps, or betrayed if the party turns against them — though such betrayal costs, for hags hold grudges across lifetimes. They know what truly waits in the cradle below: the atropal's nature, its hunger, and the price of its waking. A genuine bargain struck with them grants the party one true answer about the cradle and safe passage past their works; refusal or treachery brings their malice down upon the expedition, and they will hunt the party through the tomb's depths with supernatural persistence."
    }
  ],
  "locations": [
    {
      "name": "Port Nyanzaru",
      "description": "The last great city of Chult — a riot of colour, drums, and commerce ruled by seven merchant princes. Dinosaurs haul cargo through the streets and race for fortunes on festival days. Beyond its walls: harbour forts, a leper colony, and three hundred miles of jungle that eats expeditions whole. The seven princes command rival trade monopolies and jealously guard their influence: Wakanga O'tamu (magic and lore, urbane collector of secrets); Captain Thorne (arms and mercenaries, gruff and pragmatic); Zhanthi (spices and luxuries, vain and status-obsessed); Ijek (slaves and labour, cruel and calculating); Lashandra (gems and jewellery, shrewd negotiator with a gambling addiction); Naja Dagalond (textiles and dyes, motherly but ruthless in business); and Kwayothé (food and agriculture, jovial drunkard hiding sharp accounting). Each prince controls a trading house, commands private soldiers, and views the party's expedition as either opportunity or threat. Favour one, and the others grow distant. Betray one's interests, and they conspire against the heroes before departure."
    },
    {
      "name": "Camp Vengeance",
      "description": "The Order of the Gauntlet's forward camp on the River Soshenstar, commanded by Captain Harwin of Waterdeep — a grizzled veteran who maintains discipline even as sickness spreads and supplies dwindle. The camp is a palisade of sharpened logs, sick soldiers, and grim resolve. Each night, undead rise from the river in organized waves, attacking from the eastern bank in coordinated masses rather than random stragglers — suggesting something intelligent commands them from upriver. The undead surge nightly because a necromantic anchor buried beneath the camp (planted by Thayan scouts) draws them; destroying it requires investigation and a dangerous midnight expedition into the graveyard the camp occupies. Harwin's debt to those who save the camp or bring news of the undead's source runs deep: the party can call in canoe escorts for future river travel, reinforcements for a critical assault on the Yuan-ti or the tomb, or magical aid in the form of healing potions and clerical blessings. The commander respects strength and honesty; help him, and he becomes a reliable ally in Chult's interior."
    },
    {
      "name": "Firefinger",
      "description": "A lone spire of red rock rising above the jungle canopy, home to a flock of murderous pterafolk. They keep trophies from every traveller they've dropped from a thousand feet — including a stolen weretiger mask."
    },
    {
      "name": "Kir Sabal",
      "description": "A monastery carved into a cliff face, reachable only by air or a heart-stopping climb, home to a colony of aarakocra. They shelter the last heirs of Omu's royal line: Prince Mala and her younger brother Prince Ashok, the sole survivors of the kingdom's fall. Both are cautious of outsiders but bound by oath to aid those who would restore their god's honour and destroy the source of Chult's curse. Mala leads the monastery's warriors and judges the party's worthiness; Ashok tends the sacred aviary where the Dance of the Seven Winds was taught to Omu's ancient kings. EARNING THE DANCE: The aarakocra gift the Dance of the Seven Winds (granting limited flight for up to three days) only to those the princes deem worthy. Worthiness is tested through three trials set by Mala: (1) Speak truly about your intentions for the Soulmonger — those who seek to seize or study it rather than destroy it are refused and barred from the monastery; (2) Demonstrate courage and honour in the aarakocra's presence — cowardice or cruelty disqualifies the seeker; (3) Prove your commitment to Omu's restoration and Chult's liberation — mercenaries, agents of foreign powers, or those who serve colonial masters are turned away. Those who pass all three trials receive Ashok's blessing: he teaches them the Dance personally over three hours, and each member of the party may fly for up to three days without magical aid. The gift is given freely, not purchased, but may be revoked if the party betrays the princes' trust or allies with the yuan-ti or Red Wizards after receiving it. If the party aids Mala and Ashok in a crisis (yuan-ti assassination attempt, rival predator threat, or recovery of a lost aarakocra), the brothers swear a blood-oath to aid the heroes in the tomb itself — they cannot descend into Acererak's lair but may provide aerial rescue, message relay, or one critical supply drop during the tomb's descent."
    },
    {
      "name": "Orolunga",
      "description": "A ruined ziggurat swallowed by green, its stepped terraces patrolled by spectral handmaidens. The ascent to Saja N'baza's summit demands five ascending trials — each a riddle or spirit-test that probes the seeker's worth. RIDDLE MECHANICS: The five guardians are bound to specific terraces: (1) Terrace of Memory (riddle on the nature of what is forgotten), (2) Terrace of Intent (riddle on the difference between wish and deed), (3) Terrace of Sacrifice (riddle on the cost of knowledge), (4) Terrace of Honesty (riddle on truth in shadow), (5) Terrace of Passage (final riddle on worthiness itself). Each riddle must be answered with genuine insight, not mere wordplay — Saja's handmaidens judge the answer's truth, and a weak or dishonest answer causes that terrace's guardian to bar re-entry for one full day and night, forcing the seeker to return later or find an alternate path. Three failed answers across all five terraces trigger a curse: the ziggurat's vines animate and entangle the intruder, holding them for 1d4 hours before release — a costly delay that tests patience as much as wit. Once all five riddles are answered truthfully, the seeker reaches the summit chamber where Saja N'baza coils around her scrying pool. SAJA'S PRICE: She does not answer directly — she demands one of three prices. (1) A SECRET: Tell her something true that the seeker has never spoken aloud — a shame, a fear, a desire — and she will answer one question about Omu's location, the Soulmonger's nature, or the path through the tomb. The secret must be genuine; she reads hearts and will know if the seeker lies. (2) A MEMORY: Surrender a treasured memory — the seeker forgets the memory forever, but Saja grants two answers instead of one. (3) A GUIDE: Pledge one of the seeker's companions to guard her ziggurat for one full lunar cycle after the Soulmonger is destroyed, and Saja grants three answers plus a blessing: the guardian is immune to charm and mind-affecting magic while bound to the ziggurat, and may never be harmed within its walls. If the party cannot or will not meet her price, she will not answer — though she may drop hints through her handmaidens if she senses genuine good intent. Most expeditions receive only one answer: the location of Omu, delivered through a riddle that must itself be solved. Some, who pay dearly, learn the Soulmonger draws power from both the nine puzzle cubes AND the atropal's essence — and that only by severing both simultaneously can the curse be permanently broken."
    },
    {
      "name": "Omu",
      "description": "The forgotten city. Once the jewel of Chult, cursed when its people abandoned their gods — now a drowned ruin in a sunken valley, ruled by a tyrannosaurus the kobolds call the King of Feathers. Nine shrines to nine dead trickster gods hide nine puzzle cubes. The city's geography follows a broken mandala: I'jin's Shrine (caprice and impulse) lies in the eastern ruin-heap where paths loop and confound; Kubazan's Temple stands in the gardens north of the palace, where strength-tests mar ancient stone; Moa's Sanctum sits west in a half-buried tower, silent and warded with blindness; Nangnang's Hoard-Grotto occupies the southern cliff-face, defended by traps that reward greed with death; Obo'laka's Ritual Chamber marks the centre beneath the royal plaza, its sequence-locks binding the rest; Papazotl's Pride-House crowns the highest ruin to the northeast, where only the worthy ascend; Shagambi's Justice-Seat dwells in the western cemetery, where old wrongs sleep unquiet; Unkh's Contemplation-Pool fills a sinkhole to the northwest, its murky depths unchanged for centuries; Wongo's Madness-Shrine clings to the ravine's southern cliff in a frenzy of carvings and bone. The Royal Palace dominates the central plateau, and beneath it coils the Fane of the Night Serpent — serpent-temple and stronghold of Ras Nsi."
    },
    {
      "name": "Fane of the Night Serpent",
      "description": "The yuan-ti stronghold beneath Omu — a labyrinth of breeding pits, sacrifice pools, and hissing devotion to Dendar the Night Serpent. The stronghold divides into three main tiers: the Upper Fangs (guard barracks, ritual chambers, and sentry posts where yuan-ti malisons keep watch over approaches); the Breeding Pits (writhing pools where eggs incubate and spawn-servants are gestated — hot, humid, and alive with serpent life); and the Sunken Sanctum, where Ras Nsi rules from his throne room at the deepest point. Ras Nsi dwells in an obsidian chamber beneath a vaulted roof carved with the profile of Dendar; the ninth puzzle cube rests upon an altar of coiled serpentine statuary directly before his throne, both shrine and seat of power combined. The cube is not worn upon his person but bound by ritual chains to the altar — removing it requires defeating Ras Nsi, negotiating his oath, or finding an alternate path around the Sunken Sanctum entirely. Smaller shrines to darker aspects of the Night Serpent cult dot the lower tier, and a central columned hall connects all three sections — a natural battleground where the party's infiltration or assault will likely pivot. Ras Nsi cannot be easily surprised here; the yuan-ti of the Fane answer to him absolutely and will defend their lord and the cube with fanatic devotion unless he orders otherwise."
    },
    {
      "name": "Tomb of the Nine Gods",
      "description": "Acererak's masterpiece, buried beneath Omu: a six-level descent of traps that reward greed with death, halls that shift and reconfigure between visits (forcing recursive navigation if players backtrack or are separated), and the sepulchres of Omu's nine murdered gods. Each level is ruled by one god and embodies that god's nature in its trials: Level One: I'jin's Caprice (paths and stairs change, nothing is where it was), Level Two: Kubazan's Bold Wildness (tests of strength and reckless valour), Level Three: Moa's Honesty (invisible threats, truth-or-death riddles), Level Four: Nangnang's Selfishness (wealth-traps, solo corridors, betrayal-doors), Level Five: Obo'laka's Ritual (locked sequences, pattern-recognition, order or death), Level Six: Papazotl's Pride (the god's own sepulchre, where Acererak's servants wait — no traps here, only guardians worthy of the archlich's presence). At the tomb's heart hangs the Soulmonger, and beneath it a cradle no light should ever have touched. The tomb's halls rearrange whenever all nine puzzle cubes leave their shrines—corridors shift, old paths close, and new ones open, forcing the party to navigate by riddle, memory, and the guidance of the trickster spirits whose cubes they carry."
    }
  ],
  "rules": [
    "This campaign is adapted from Tomb of Annihilation. Stay true to the major plot beats, factions, and NPCs but adapt freely to the player's choices and character.",
    "The death curse is the campaign's clock — make it felt through people, not mechanics: Syndra visibly worse at each meeting, funerals in Port Nyanzaru, desperate letters from families of the withering. If the player character was ever raised from the dead, the curse touches them personally. SYNDRA'S DETERIORATION TIMELINE: At Act I start, Syndra is grey-skinned and sunken-eyed but mobile and commanding. By Act II start (The Forgotten City), she is noticeably frail, moving slowly, and confined to her lodgings in Port Nyanzaru or a traveling shelter — she no longer meets the party in public. By Act III start (The Tomb of the Nine Gods), she is bedridden and cannot leave her lodgings; she communicates only by sending or through magical scrying. If the party does not reach the Soulmonger's chamber and begin the final descent before the mid-point of Act III, Syndra dies during that act — her withering accelerates beyond even magical remedy. If she dies before the party escapes the tomb, she can no longer cast the promised teleportation ritual to extract them; the party must find their own path out of the Tomb of the Nine Gods or perish within it. This transforms the final ascent from a victory lap into a desperate race against the tomb's collapse and Acererak's servants.",
    "Chult itself is an antagonist. Heat, torrential rain, insects, disease, quicksand, and things with teeth — travel should never feel free. But the jungle is also gorgeous and alive; wonder and dread in equal measure.",
    "Port Nyanzaru and the Chultans are vibrant, sophisticated, and proudly independent — never portray them as primitive. Foreign colonial powers lost their grip on Chult; the locals remember why.",
    "THE NINE TRICKSTER GODS OF OMU (canonical — use these names and natures everywhere shrines, puzzle cubes, or tomb spirits appear). Each shrine, cube, and spirit expresses its god's character — puzzles and hauntings alike should FEEL like the god they belong to. SHRINE LOCATIONS AND MAPPINGS IN OMU: I'jin the almiraj (Shrine of Caprice, northeast platform accessible by boulder-rolling puzzle; puzzle cube grants unpredictable re-rolls once per session) — Kubazan the froghemoth (Shrine of Bold Wildness, eastern ruin-heap with strength-test trials; cube grants advantage on Athletics checks once per day) — Moa the jaculi (Shrine of Honesty, western half-buried tower warded with blindness-magic; cube reveals one hidden truth when held to eyes, consuming the revelation) — Nangnang the grung (Hoard-Grotto, southern cliff-face defended by greed-traps that reward taking with death; cube allows the bearer to sense wealth within 60 feet once per day) — Obo'laka the zorbo (Ritual Chamber, center beneath the royal plaza with sequence-locks binding the rest; cube grants perfect recall of one sequence or pattern witnessed, usable once per session) — Papazotl the eblis (Pride-House, highest ruin to the northeast where only the worthy ascend via trial of valour; cube grants advantage on Charisma checks against proud or vain creatures once per day) — Shagambi the kamadan (Justice-Seat, western cemetery where old wrongs sleep unquiet; cube allows the bearer to sense injustice and active curses within 60 feet) — Unkh the flail snail (Contemplation-Pool, sinkhole to the northwest with murky depths unchanged for centuries; cube grants one hour of total clarity on a confused or chaotic decision once per session) — Wongo the su-monster (Madness-Shrine, ravine's southern cliff in a frenzy of carvings and bone; cube grants immunity to fear until the next dawn, consumed on use). The Royal Palace dominates the central plateau, and beneath it coils the Fane of the Night Serpent — serpent-temple and stronghold of Ras Nsi, where the ninth cube rests upon his obsidian throne.",
    "Acererak's tomb kills fairly: telegraph every trap, reward paranoia and cleverness, and let deaths feel earned, never arbitrary. The tomb is a dialogue between the player and a sadistic genius who wants an audience."
  ],
